The release archives are built with every media dependency BUNDLED,
WITH_GPL=OFF and WITH_NONFREE=OFF. x264, x265, Rubberband, VagueDenoiser's
GPL path and FDK-AAC are not in those archives. A build that turns either
switch on is a different binary and is not described here.

This archive does not include a kit of relocatable object files. The way
provided to rebuild or replace this library is to compile its corresponding
source. That source is the Git tag of the release, checked out with its
submodules:

  git clone --recursive --branch <tag> https://github.com/StormByte-Suite/StormByte-Multimedia.git

The automatic GitHub source zip of the tag does not contain the submodules.
It is not, by itself, the complete corresponding source of this binary.
The release page is:

  https://github.com/StormByte-Suite/StormByte-Multimedia/releases/tag/1.0.0

Rebuild that tag with the same switches this archive used:

  -DWITH_FFMPEG=BUNDLED -DWITH_ZIMG=BUNDLED -DWITH_EBUR128=BUNDLED
  -DWITH_VMAF=BUNDLED -DWITH_OCR=BUNDLED -DWITH_TESSDATA=BUNDLED
  -DWITH_STORMBYTE=BUNDLED -DWITH_GPL=OFF -DWITH_NONFREE=OFF
  -DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=ON

Substituting the resulting shared library is the relink method provided
with this binary. FFmpeg and the codec plugins are compiled in (static
absorb). They are not separate shared libraries in the zip. The C++ runtime
is not in this archive. The dynamic loader still has to provide libc++,
libstdc++ or the MSVC CRT, whichever this build was linked against.

The commercial option in LICENSE covers original StormByte-Multimedia
source only. It does not relicense the compiled-in components below. A
binary that contains them still has to meet their licenses, including the
LGPL terms for FFmpeg, LAME and (on Windows) pthreads-win32.

